<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SharingRules xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
      THIS COMMENT IS INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT ON PURPOSE - a comment above the root breaks sf
      at source conversion. This file was previously an empty <SharingRules/> root; nothing is
      being removed.

      🔴 WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. Case OWD in this org is PRIVATE (queried from
      EntityDefinition 2026-08-22; the repo file had drifted to ReadWriteTransfer behind the
      old blanket ignore, and has been corrected). A Delinquency Case is owned by ONE property
      manager, so with no sharing rule:
        - no other PM can work it,
        - a principal cannot open the Case they are being asked to approve a write-off on,
        - and any approval-triggered write fails for every approver who does not OWN the record.
      That last one is not hypothetical on this project - it has already happened once, and it
      was invisible because SYSTEM_MODE lifts CRUD and FLS but NEVER sharing, and the DML was
      running with allOrNone = false, which swallowed the failure. Principals therefore get
      Edit, not Read: a write-off decision writes to the Case.

      🔴 THIS DOES NOT COVER THE NIGHTLY JOB. Sharing rules share to groups; the sync's
      never-duplicate guard must see open Cases owned by ANYONE, including users outside both
      groups. If that read is sharing-filtered it does not merely miss a Case - it INVERTS the
      feature into a duplicate-maker, silently. The fix belongs in the integration permission
      set (viewAllRecords + modifyAllRecords on Case, scoped to the integration principal
      only), not here. See HUB FILE REQUESTS.

      🔴 DEPLOY ONE RULE AT A TIME - see the note in
      sharingRules/Receivables_Summary__c.sharingRules-meta.xml. A check-only dry run cannot prove this.

      CRITERIA CHOICE. booleanFilter is "1 OR 2" so the rule catches both a Case the sync
      created (Yardi_AR_Id__c always stamped) and one a PM raised by hand before any sync row
      existed (Resolution_Path__c set, external id blank). Record Type would be the obvious
      criterion but its metadata value format is unverified in this repo and there is no Case
      sharing rule to copy - these two fields are verified to exist and are non-formula.
    -->
    <sharingCriteriaRules>
        <fullName>Delinquency_Cases_PM_Team_Edit</fullName>
        <accessLevel>Edit</accessLevel>
        <booleanFilter>1 OR 2</booleanFilter>
        <description>Lets the whole Property Management team work any delinquency Case, not only the ones they personally own. Cover, handover and escalation all depend on a second PM being able to comment and advance the stage.</description>
        <label>Delinquency Cases PM Team Edit</label>
        <criteriaItems>
            <field>Yardi_AR_Id__c</field>
            <operation>notEqual</operation>
            <value></value>
        </criteriaItems>
        <criteriaItems>
            <field>Resolution_Path__c</field>
            <operation>notEqual</operation>
            <value></value>
        </criteriaItems>
        <sharedTo>
            <group>DPEG_Property_Mgmt_Team</group>
        </sharedTo>
    </sharingCriteriaRules>
    <sharingCriteriaRules>
        <fullName>Delinquency_Cases_Principals_Edit</fullName>
        <accessLevel>Edit</accessLevel>
        <booleanFilter>1 OR 2</booleanFilter>
        <description>Edit rather than Read, deliberately. A principal is the write-off approver, and an approval that writes back to a Case they do not own fails silently without record-level edit access - the exact incident this project has already had. Read access alone would let them open the Case and then quietly refuse the decision.</description>
        <label>Delinquency Cases Principals Edit</label>
        <criteriaItems>
            <field>Yardi_AR_Id__c</field>
            <operation>notEqual</operation>
            <value></value>
        </criteriaItems>
        <criteriaItems>
            <field>Resolution_Path__c</field>
            <operation>notEqual</operation>
            <value></value>
        </criteriaItems>
        <sharedTo>
            <group>Principals</group>
        </sharedTo>
    </sharingCriteriaRules>
</SharingRules>
